 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Kostya has told the boss he broke the mirror, but Nastya won't let him take the blame. Both of them are standing in front of Barinov. Here is what they say, in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Шеф, это я сломал зеркало машины.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, совсем не верьте ему!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Это я сама сломала зеркало!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Вы оба сломали моё зеркало?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я ехала на велосипеде и задела.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Я просто хотел защитить Настю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, простите нас обоих, пожалуйста!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я два дня ругался с соседкой!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Идите работать, вы оба настоящие инвалиды!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Настя, спасибо, ты поступила очень смело.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "И ты меня красиво защитил, Костя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Может, ты сегодня придёшь в гости?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "С огромным удовольствием приду к тебе!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now the confession again, with time to look at each line.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Шеф, это я сломал зеркало машины.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, I broke your car mirror.' Let us break down the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "зеркало",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "машины",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'the car mirror', and the term for car takes a different ending when it means 'of the car'. Kostya's verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "сломал",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ends in a man's past form.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, совсем не верьте ему!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, do not believe him at all!' In Russian you believe 'to' a person, so 'him' changes shape to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "ему",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". Notice ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "совсем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning at all, makes the order stronger.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Это я сама сломала зеркало!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I broke your side mirror myself!' Let us look at the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "сама",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'myself', in a woman's form. Nastya adds it so that nobody can doubt it was her.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Вы оба сломали моё зеркало?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Did you both break my mirror together?' Let us inspect the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "оба",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'both', and with it the plural ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Вы",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " clearly means 'you two'. The verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "сломали",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has the plural ending.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я ехала на велосипеде и задела.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I was riding a bicycle and grazed it.' The verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "ехала",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'was riding', with a woman's ending. We hear ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "на",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "велосипеде",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'on a bicycle', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "задела",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'brushed against'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Я просто хотел защитить Настю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I was simply trying to protect Nastya, chef.' The verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "защитить",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'to protect'. The name ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Настю",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " changes its ending because she is the one protected.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, простите нас обоих, пожалуйста!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, please forgive both of us!' The expression ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "нас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "обоих",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'we both' in the form for the people being forgiven. The verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "простите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " echoes the term Russians use for 'excuse me'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я два дня ругался с соседкой!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I was fighting with the neighbor for two days!' After 'two', the term for day becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "дня",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". A woman neighbor is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "соседкой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", and ",
