  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "с",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "соседкой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'with the neighbor'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Идите работать, вы оба настоящие инвалиды!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Go back to work, you clumsy people!' The term ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "инвалиды",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'disabled people', and here it is a rude way of calling them hopeless. The expression ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Идите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "работать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the plural order plus the plain verb.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Настя, спасибо, ты поступила очень смело.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Nastya, thank you, you acted very bravely.' The term ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "смело",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
